<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1685">1685</a>–<a href="/years/1740">1740</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles VI was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> from 1711 to 1740 and the last male <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> of the senior line. A claimant to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, he received instead the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1713–1714, and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1718. He created the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a> in 1719 and spent years winning acceptance of the Pragmatic Sanction for his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="491" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Gottfried Auerbach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Gottfried_Auerbach_004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Archduke Charles was born on 1 October 1685, the second son of Emperor Leopold I and his third wife, Eleonore Magdalene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a>. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> in 1700 without a direct heir, he declared himself King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> lasted almost 14 years against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s grandson Philip, Duke of Anjou. Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, Ireland and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> backed Charles, and in Spain his supporters were concentrated in the Crown of Aragon.</p><p>He landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1705 and held power only in Catalonia until the death of his brother Joseph I called him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. In 1708 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-christine-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Elisabeth Christine of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, unwilling to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> united again, withdrew its support, and the Treaties of Utrecht, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt">Rastatt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> recognised Philip as king of Spain while ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, the Austrian Netherlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> to Austria. In the Austro-Turkish War of 1716 to 1718 he added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and established direct Austrian rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>. The War of the Polish Succession ended in 1738 with Charles giving Naples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to Don Carlos in exchange for Parma and Tuscany. His last Turkish war, begun in 1737 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-belgrade">Treaty of Belgrade</a> in 1739, in which he gave up central Serbia and Oltenia.</p><p>Charles was a devout Catholic who supported the restoration of Catholic church structures in lands freed from Ottoman rule, and he confirmed older privileges of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> subjects. Having no surviving son, he issued the Pragmatic Sanction of 19 April 1713, abolishing male-only succession and declaring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands indivisible, although the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> approved it only in 1723. He spent the rest of his reign seeking recognition from other powers, and agreed to close the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostend-company">Ostend Company</a>, which he had founded in 1722, to win <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s assent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles fell ill after a hunting trip in October 1740 and died on 20 October in the Hofburg; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> wrote that he died after eating death cap mushrooms. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> had to defend her inheritance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> against a coalition of states, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, that had all been parties to the sanction. She kept her crown and most of her lands but lost Silesia to Prussia and Parma to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. At his death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands were deeply in debt, the exchequer held only 100,000 florins, and the Habsburg male line was extinct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vienna, Prague and later Pest grew as court, administrative and commercial centres, and <b>Charles VI</b> declared Trieste and Fiume free ports in 1719 to open an outlet to the Adriatic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On 23 January 1719 Emperor <b>Charles VI</b> united the two and raised them to an imperial principality named Liechtenstein for Prince Anton Florian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the War of the Spanish Succession, the Treaties of Utrecht and Rastatt in 1713–1714 transferred the Spanish Netherlands to <b>Emperor Charles VI</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/austrian-netherlands">Austrian Netherlands</a></li><li><blockquote>Rather than returning it to Hungary, <b>Charles VI</b> kept it as a crown domain under military and court administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/banat-of-temeswar">Banat of Temeswar</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1719 <b>Emperor Charles VI</b> united the two as the Principality of Liechtenstein, of which Vaduz is the capital today.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-vaduz">County of Vaduz</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1719 <b>Emperor Charles VI</b> united Schellenberg and Vaduz as the Principality of Liechtenstein, where it survives as the Unterland.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-schellenberg">Lordship of Schellenberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1720" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1720">What was here in 1720? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
